%% Answer plans for every past T6 question — RN volume only.

## Every past question as an answer plan

Use these plans in the last fortnight: read the question, say the plan aloud from memory, then check. Each plan is the skeleton of the full model answer in T6QA.

**2016 Q4 — Pros and cons of gender quota; impact in Pakistan.** Quota defined; CEDAW 4(1), Art. 25(3); 130+ countries. Types: reserved seats (60; Bangladesh 50), candidate quotas (5% 2017; France 2000), party quotas. Pros: fast track (Dahlerup; Pakistan 1990s 2–6 women vs 20%+ in 2002); barriers not merit; descriptive representation (Pitkin 1967); policy (Chattopadhyay and Duflo 2004); role models; critical mass (Kanter 1977; 30%). Cons: merit and tokenism (answer: merit distorted; Sweden's "mediocre men"); proxies (*biwi-beti-behen*), stigma, elite capture; ceiling; root causes. Compare figure. Pakistan gains: LFO 2002 (60, 17, 128), 36,000 councillors, caucus 2008 laws (2010, 2011, 2012, 2016), FAFEN activity. Limits: party lists, no constituency, 11 general-seat women (2024), no ministers (2025), local cuts after 2013. View: design not principle; reforms (direct election, winnable seats, sanctions, 33% local).

**2017 Q4 — Women's active participation stabilises democracy and boosts development; Pakistani realities.** Participation defined; "active" vs nominal; 46.1% of voters, 43% vs 52% turnout, 11 + 60. Democracy: legitimacy (UDHR 21, CEDAW 7); cross-party caucus; UNSCR 1325 (2000). Development: health, education, water (Chattopadhyay and Duflo); women's economic laws (2010, Sindh 2018, 2020); LFPR 24.4%. Cycle figure. Evidence: caucus laws, BISP 2008, 2001 councillors. Realities: literacy 53%, 9.94m voter gap; purdah, block votes, local bans; feudal and *biradari* power; dynasties; party dependence; military interventions (1985 and 2002 seats under military rulers); weak enforcement; zero ministers 2025. Reforms (CNICs, s. 9, direct election, party democracy, 33% local, education and work, safety, equal resources). Real participation, not token presence.

**2018 Q7 — "Women cover half the sky": equal numbers in all public spheres, especially politics?** Saying (Mao); SDG 5.5; Beijing Area G. Pakistan today: about one-fifth of NA, 11 general seats, zero ministers 2025, 10% job quota, Ayesha Malik 2022 — pyramid figure. For parity: justice (UDHR 21, CEDAW 7, Arts 25, 34; Jinnah 1944); interests (Phillips 1995); better decisions; evidence (2010, 2016 laws; India water); role models; critical mass; France 2000, Rwanda 60%+, Nepal 35% (2026). Against rigid parity: merit and choice; unfillable seats; women not one group; proxies. Politics matters most (sets all budgets and laws). Other spheres: bureaucracy, judiciary, boards (one woman director; Norway 40%), media. Position: equal opportunity, quotas as a bridge to parity, power not just numbers, diversity among women.

**2019 Q8(a) — Short note: suffragist movement.** (~555 words.) Suffrage = right to vote; suffragists vs suffragettes (*Daily Mail* 1906); separate spheres, coverture vs natural rights, democracy; Wollstonecraft 1792. USA: Seneca Falls 1848 (Stanton, Mott), 15th Amendment 1870, NWSA and AWSA 1869, NAWSA 1890, Wyoming 1869, Alice Paul, 19th Amendment 1920, VRA 1965. Britain: Mill 1866/1869, NUWSS 1897 (Fawcett), WSPU 1903 (Pankhurst), Cat and Mouse 1913, Davison 1913, 1918 (over 30), 1928 (21). Timeline figure. World: New Zealand 1893 (Sheppard), Finland 1906. South Asia: 1917 (WIA, Naidu), Madras 1921, 1935 Act, Pakistan 1947, Fatima Jinnah 1965, two women in the CA. Assessment: class and race limits; second wave; 9.94m gap; necessary but not sufficient.

**2020 Q5 — "Pull each other up": useful or preferential treatment?** Definition; useful if fair. Gap: zero ministers 2025; leaky pipeline (women majority of medical graduates). Barriers: glass ceiling, labyrinth (Eagly and Carli 2007), old boys' networks, double bind, care. Useful: role models (Beaman 2012); mentoring, sponsorship (over-mentored, under-sponsored), networks — mapping figure; ending tokenism (Kanter; 30%); changing policies (harassment, childcare); caucus laws. Objection: merit, resentment, nepotism fears. Doubts: unfair burden, solidarity assumed, queen bee (1974). Pakistan: Benazir (women's police stations 1994; double bind), caucus, 10% quota, Ayesha Malik 2022, Tehmina Janjua 2017. Line: corrective, not preferential — within transparent procedures, plus quotas, childcare and harassment enforcement.

**2021 Q8 — Is the 17% quota showing results? How quotas can mainstream women.** Quota and mainstreaming defined (Beijing 1995 gender mainstreaming). LFO 2002: 60 of 342 ≈ 17%; PR by province; 336 after 2018. Results: numbers (2–6 in 1990s → one-fifth; 36,000 councillors; fast track); laws (2010, 2011, 2012, 2016, provincial DV 2013–21); agenda, FAFEN/PILDAT activity, symbols (Fahmida Mirza 2008, Maryam Nawaz 2024). Grid figure. Limits: party dependence and no constituency (2024–25 reserved-seat dispute); 11 general seats (4%), 5% rule weak, ceiling; soft portfolios, zero ministers 2025, elite capture, local cuts after 2013. Rethink: rotating or dual-member constituencies; interim districts with funds; candidate quota to 33% in winnable seats; party elections for lists; 33% local pipeline; equal resources; Nepal 35%, India's panchayats. Results real but partial; design for mainstreaming.

**2022 Q5 — Main issues faced by women as representatives.** Opinion: present but not powerful. Entry: 336/60, 11 general (2024), Senate 17, provinces 132. Structural: "nominated" label; no constituency, no route to general seats. Party: dependence and discipline (2024–25 dispute); relatives on lists, elite capture (rural, home-based, minority women absent). Institutional: unequal funds and offices; soft portfolios (Hina Rabbani Khar 2011 exception); few committee chairs; zero ministers 2025. Social: sexism in the House, online abuse, rare discipline; family duties, no day-care; capacity. Grid figure. Response: caucus 2008 (Fahmida Mirza) → 2010, 2011, 2012, 2016 laws; FAFEN activity. Reforms: direct election, transparent lists, equal funds, key committees and ministries, codes of conduct, day-care, research support. Opinion: dependence is the deepest issue.

**2024 Q7 — Participation a human right? Obstacles for women politicians; impact of quotas.** Yes: UDHR 21 (1948), ICCPR 25 (1966; Pakistan 2010), CEDAW 7 and 4(1) (Pakistan 1996), General Comment 25 (1996), SDG 5.5; Arts 17, 25, 32, 34, 51, 59, 106; Elections Act ss. 206 and 9; the state must remove obstacles. Obstacles to careers: party gatekeeping and electables (306 contested, 11 won; 5% says nothing on winnable seats); money and ignored spending limits; norms, consent, care, double bind; violence (Benazir 2007), online abuse (PECA 2016); FPTP and dynasty as the only route. Flow figure. Quota impact: entry (1990s vs 2002; 36,000 councillors; 5%); careers — positive (experience, caucus laws) and negative (party lists, no constituency, ceiling, stigma, zero ministers 2025). Rights-based reforms.

**2025 Q5 — Historical context and evolution of women's quota in Pakistan.** Symbolic → substantial → not yet empowering. Context: exclusion; CEDAW 4(1); Art. 25(3); women in the Pakistan Movement. 1935 Act (6 million women; reserved seats; Jahanara Shahnawaz, Round Table, Punjab 1937). 1947: 2 women in the CA. 1956: 10 (5 + 5). 1962: 6, indirect (Ayub; Fatima Jinnah 1965). 1973: 10 for ten years or three elections — "temporary" bridge removed too soon. 1985: 20 (Zia; Hudood 1979, Evidence 1984, WAF 1981). 1988–2002 lapse: 1990, 1993, 1997, 2–6 women, 97% male, even under Benazir. 2000–02: Devolution/LGO 2001 33%, 36,000; LFO 2002 (342; 60; 17; 128), 17th Amendment 2003; NCSW 2000, National Policy 2002; under 3% → over 20%. 2008 caucus; 2010 18th Amendment; 2013+ local cuts; 2017 Elections Act (5%, 10%); 2018 25th Amendment (336, 60 kept). Timeline figure. Gains vs gaps (2024–25 dispute). Next: direct election, 33% candidate quota, 33% local, transparent lists; Nepal 35% (2026).
